&lt;h2&gt;


  Caveats, so you can trust the rest
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dollar figures use 1 credit = $0.01, the rate of the smallest credit pack ($10 for 1,000 credits). Monthly plans and larger packs cost the same or less per credit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For a few models (Kling 3.0, VEO 3.1, Hailuo 2.3) the published resolution is the one we measured on the output, not the setting, because the setting is not always honored.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Prices change. Every row has a snapshot date. In the 30-day window above, every failed job that had been charged was refunded.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Price says nothing about quality. Run the same prompt on two or three models before you commit a budget.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where Kreavo fits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Kreavo is an AI UGC video tool for ecommerce brands: you keep one AI influencer, add your product photo, and render the scene on whichever of these models fits the budget. The full table, the per-second grid and two CSV files live on the &lt;a href="https://kreavo.ai/ai-video-generation-cost-index" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI video generation cost index&lt;/a&gt;, with the snapshot date on every row.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you spot a number that looks wrong, leave a comment with the model, length and resolution, and we will check it against the formula.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Automating Multi‑Channel Content Publishing with a Simple Node Script
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I added a lightweight Node / npm‑based generator that creates platform‑specific files (Markdown for Medium/Substack, JSON for Bluesky) from a single source template. The change cuts manual copy‑paste time by ~80 % and keeps publishing metadata in sync.  &lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My weekly sprint for the &lt;strong&gt;content‑automation&lt;/strong&gt; project required me to publish the same article on three platforms:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Medium – expects a &lt;code&gt;.md&lt;/code&gt; file with front‑matter.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Substack – also consumes Markdown but with a slightly different header format.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bluesky – requires a JSON payload with an array of “post” objects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Previously I was manually copying the article body into each format, adjusting headings, and updating a &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; file by hand. The symptom was obvious in the commit history: a flood of almost‑identical markdown files and a growing &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; drift. The biggest pain point showed up as a CI failure when the &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; flag &lt;code&gt;medium_generated&lt;/code&gt; was still &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt; after I had already pushed the Medium post.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"medium_generated"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"substack_generated"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The mismatch caused the CI pipeline to abort, and I realized the process needed automation.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first attempt was to write a Bash one‑liner that &lt;code&gt;sed&lt;/code&gt;‑ed the source markdown into the different formats and then &lt;code&gt;jq&lt;/code&gt;‑ed the JSON for Bluesky. The script looked like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;#!/usr/bin/env bash&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;SRC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"content/2026/10/26/content-automation/article.md"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Medium&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'s/^# /## /'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$SRC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"content/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt; +%Y/%m/%d&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/content-automation/medium_en.md"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Substack (same as Medium for now)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"content/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt; +%Y/%m/%d&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/content-automation/medium_en.md"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
   &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"content/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt; +%Y/%m/%d&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/content-automation/substack_en.md"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Bluesky JSON&lt;/span&gt;
jq &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--arg&lt;/span&gt; text &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cat&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$SRC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'[{type:"progress", text:$text}]'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
   &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"content/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt; +%Y/%m/%d&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/content-automation/bluesky_en.json"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It &lt;em&gt;worked&lt;/em&gt; for a single article, but it quickly broke:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Encoding issues&lt;/strong&gt; – the &lt;code&gt;cat&lt;/code&gt; inside &lt;code&gt;jq&lt;/code&gt; mangled Unicode characters.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hard‑coded paths&lt;/strong&gt; – every new day required manual directory creation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No error handling&lt;/strong&gt; – the script silently overwrote files.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because of these flaws, the script was discarded after the first failed run.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I rewrote the generator in &lt;strong&gt;Node.js&lt;/strong&gt;, which gave me better string handling, asynchronous file I/O, and the ability to reuse a single source template (&lt;code&gt;article.template.md&lt;/code&gt;). The core of the solution lives in &lt;code&gt;scripts/generate-content.js&lt;/code&gt;. Below is a trimmed version that shows the essential steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  File: &lt;code&gt;scripts/generate-content.js&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="cp"&gt;#!/usr/bin/env node
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;fs/promises&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;format&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;date-fns&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;execSync&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;child_process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ---------- Config ----------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ROOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// today&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DATE_PATH&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;yyyy/MM/dd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DEST&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ROOT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DATE_PATH&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content-automation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ---------- Helpers ----------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ensureDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mkdir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;recursive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;readTemplate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;templates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;article.template.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ---------- Main ----------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ensureDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DEST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;readTemplate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 1️⃣ Medium (adds front‑matter)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;medium&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`---\ntitle: "Weekly Build‑in‑Public: Content‑Automation Sprint"\ndate: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;yyyy-MM-dd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;\n---\n\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DEST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;medium_en.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;medium&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;✅ medium_en.md generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 2️⃣ Substack (different front‑matter style)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;substack&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`---\nsubject: "Weekly Build‑in‑Public: Content‑Automation Sprint"\n---\n\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DEST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;substack_en.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;substack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;✅ substack_en.md generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 3️⃣ Bluesky JSON payload&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;bluesky&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;progress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\\&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// escape newlines for JSON string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DEST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;bluesky_en.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;bluesky&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;✅ bluesky_en.json generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 4️⃣ Update metadata.json atomically&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ROOT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;metadata.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaRaw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaRaw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;medium_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;substack_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;bluesky_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;✅ metadata.json updated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 5️⃣ Optional: auto‑commit the changes (CI‑friendly)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;execSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;git add .&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;stdio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;inherit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;execSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`git commit -m "chore(content): auto‑generate &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;yyyy-MM-dd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;stdio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;inherit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h4&gt;


  Why this works
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Decision&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Reason&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Node.js + ES modules&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Native async/await, clean import syntax, no external runtime needed on CI.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;fs/promises&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Guarantees non‑blocking I/O, easier error handling with &lt;code&gt;try/catch&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;date-fns&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Small footprint for date formatting; avoids heavy Moment.js.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Atomic &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; update&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reads the whole file, flips flags, writes back in one step – eliminates race conditions.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Auto‑commit&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keeps the repo in sync with CI expectations (&lt;code&gt;medium_generated&lt;/code&gt; flag).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Generated Files (excerpt)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/27/content-automation/bluesky_en.json&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"progress"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"I was halfway through a two‑week sprint on **albaida‑tickets** when I realized the dashboard I’d been polishing for days didn’t fit in anyone’s pocket. The grid of cards, the side‑b..."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/27/content-automation/medium_en.md&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (first 5 lines)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nn"&gt;---&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Weekly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Build‑in‑Public:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Content‑Automation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Sprint"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;2026-10-27&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nn"&gt;---&lt;/span&gt;

I was halfway through a two‑week sprint on &lt;span class="gs"&gt;**albaida‑tickets**&lt;/span&gt; when I realized the dashboard I’d been polishing for days didn’t fit in anyone’s pocket...
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/27/content-automation/metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (relevant section)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"pull_requests"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"releases"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"closed_issues"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"medium_generated"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"substack_generated"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"bluesky_generated"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;All three platform files are now created with a single command:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm run generate-content   &lt;span class="c"&gt;# defined in package.json as "node scripts/generate-content.js"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The CI pipeline now checks only the boolean flags in &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;. If any flag is &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;, the build fails&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/content-automation&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-10-28&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agents are good at producing HTML: test summaries, research briefs, dashboards, small prototypes. Getting that file onto a URL someone can open usually takes longer than writing it. You have to pick a host, sign up, set up a CLI or connect a repo, then wait for a build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This walkthrough uses &lt;a href="https://deed.page" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;deed.page&lt;/a&gt;, a static host built for this case. You can publish without an account, the response is JSON an agent can parse, and updates are versioned, so a script can safely overwrite its own report every night.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything below is plain &lt;code&gt;curl&lt;/code&gt;. You can run it yourself or give it to an agent as a shell tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Write the report
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cat&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; report.html &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;HTML&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'
&amp;lt;!doctype html&amp;gt;&amp;lt;html&amp;gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&amp;lt;meta charset="utf-8"&amp;gt;&amp;lt;title&amp;gt;Nightly test report&amp;lt;/title&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/head&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;body&amp;gt;&amp;lt;h1&amp;gt;Nightly test report&amp;lt;/h1&amp;gt;&amp;lt;p&amp;gt;412 passed, 3 failed.&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/body&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/html&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;HTML
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Publish it
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;SLUG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;agent-report-&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;openssl rand &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-hex&lt;/span&gt; 3&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-T&lt;/span&gt; report.html &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://deed.page/v1/sites?slug=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$SLUG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"X-Client: report-bot"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; first.json
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -T&lt;/code&gt; sends a &lt;code&gt;PUT&lt;/code&gt; with the file as the body. No API key is needed. The response looks like this (trimmed):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"ok"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://agent-report-87cc65.deed.page/"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"version"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"v1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"version_url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://v1--agent-report-87cc65.deed.page/"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"token"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"deed_tok_â€¦"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The site is live right away, and &lt;code&gt;version_url&lt;/code&gt; is a permanent link to this exact version.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;token&lt;/code&gt; is only shown once, and it's what lets you update or delete the site later. Save it to a file outside the agent's context instead of printing it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;mkdir&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-p&lt;/span&gt; ~/.config/deed-page &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;chmod &lt;/span&gt;700 ~/.config/deed-page
python3 &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"import json;print(json.load(open('first.json'))['token'])"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; ~/.config/deed-page/token
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;chmod &lt;/span&gt;600 ~/.config/deed-page/token &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;rm &lt;/span&gt;first.json
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;export &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;DEEDPAGE_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cat&lt;/span&gt; ~/.config/deed-page/token&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If an LLM is driving the shell, this matters. A token that shows up in tool output ends up in the transcript, the logs and possibly the next prompt. Keep it in a file and let the tool read it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Update it safely
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The next night the numbers change. Publish to the same slug with the token. Add &lt;code&gt;If-Match&lt;/code&gt; so the write only succeeds if the site is still at the version you expect:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'s/412 passed, 3 failed/415 passed, 0 failed/'&lt;/span&gt; report.html
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-T&lt;/span&gt; report.html &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://deed.page/v1/sites?slug=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$SLUG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Authorization: Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$DEEDPAGE_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'If-Match: "v1"'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# -&amp;gt; "version": "v2"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If two agents (or two runs of the same cron job) race, the one holding a stale version gets a &lt;code&gt;409&lt;/code&gt; instead of silently overwriting the other:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"code"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"version_conflict"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"message"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Slug agent-report-87cc65 is at v2, not v1. Re-read and retry."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"version"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"v2"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The error body tells the agent the current version, so it can re-read the site and retry. If you publish exactly the same bytes again, you get &lt;code&gt;"unchanged": true&lt;/code&gt; and no new version.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Look back and roll back
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every publish is kept as a version (the free plan keeps the last 10):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://deed.page/v1/sites/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$SLUG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/versions"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Authorization: Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$DEEDPAGE_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each entry has a &lt;code&gt;permalink&lt;/code&gt; like &lt;code&gt;https://v2--agent-report-87cc65.deed.page/&lt;/code&gt;. If tonight's report is wrong, restore an older one. The restore becomes a new version, so history is never rewritten:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-X&lt;/span&gt; POST &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://deed.page/v1/sites/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$SLUG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/versions/v1/restore"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Authorization: Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$DEEDPAGE_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# -&amp;gt; "version": "v3", content equal to v1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  5. Clean up
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-X&lt;/span&gt; DELETE &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://deed.page/v1/sites/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$SLUG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Authorization: Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$DEEDPAGE_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# -&amp;gt; {"ok":true,"deleted":"agent-report-87cc65"}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The URL returns 404 a few seconds later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  More than one file
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a folder (HTML plus CSS, images and JS), pack it and upload the tarball the same way:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;tar&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-C&lt;/span&gt; ./dist &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-czf&lt;/span&gt; site.tar.gz &lt;span class="nb"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-T&lt;/span&gt; site.tar.gz &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://deed.page/v1/sites?slug=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$SLUG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Authorization: Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$DEEDPAGE_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Clean URLs work (&lt;code&gt;/about&lt;/code&gt; serves &lt;code&gt;about.html&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;about/index.html&lt;/code&gt;), and a root &lt;code&gt;404.html&lt;/code&gt; is used for missing pages. Single-page apps can send &lt;code&gt;X-Spa: 1&lt;/code&gt; so unknown paths fall back to &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt;. A single request is capped at 4 MB, and larger sites use the manifest upload described in the &lt;a href="https://deed.page/docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;docs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Letting an agent do this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your agent runs shell commands, the steps above are all it needs. Two guardrails are worth adding:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ask before publishing.&lt;/strong&gt; A URL is public. Have the agent show what it is about to publish (file list and a preview) and wait for an explicit "yes" before running the &lt;code&gt;PUT&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Keep the token out of the conversation.&lt;/strong&gt; Read it from &lt;code&gt;~/.config/deed-page/token&lt;/code&gt; inside the tool, never echo it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For agents that speak MCP, deed.page also has an MCP endpoint at &lt;code&gt;https://deed.page/mcp&lt;/code&gt;. There's an open-source agent skill that follows both rules at &lt;a href="https://github.com/f-o-x11/deed-page-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/f-o-x11/deed-page-skill&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Limits worth knowing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The free plan allows 25 sites, 25 MB per site, 60 publishes an hour and 10 versions per site. Free sites show a small "Published with deed.page" badge. The paid plans are listed on &lt;a href="https://deed.page" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;deed.page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Full API reference: &lt;a href="https://deed.page/docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;deed.page/docs&lt;/a&gt;. There's also a plain-text version for LLMs at &lt;a href="https://deed.page/llms.txt" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;deed.page/llms.txt&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, BOB Gateway is non-custodial in the sense that BOB never takes your BTC into a company account, pools it in a shared vault, or locks it in a bridge contract against a wrapped token, as its &lt;a href="https://www.gobob.xyz/en/blog/safest-bitcoin-bridges-for-native-BTC" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;custody and trust model guide&lt;/a&gt; sets out. The honest answer still depends on direction: swapping out of BTC briefly places your coins with a single solver, while swapping into BTC keeps your money in a contract escrow that the solver cannot touch until an on-chain Bitcoin light client proves you were paid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why the label alone tells you very little
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every major service that moves native BTC calls itself non-custodial, and the label hides more than it reveals. It does not say whether your BTC passes through a validator vault, a solver's wallet, or a timelocked contract mid-swap, and each can fail differently. The useful questions are the ones BOB's own guide starts with: where does your BTC sit, who controls that address, and what happens if the other side goes quiet?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  BOB Gateway when you swap out of BTC
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You request a quote, receive a signed quote with a guaranteed minimum output, and create an order against those terms, per the &lt;a href="https://docs.gobob.xyz/gateway/technical" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gateway technical deep dive&lt;/a&gt;. You then send one Bitcoin transaction to a unique deposit address created for your order. That address belongs to the solver's own Bitcoin wallet, and this direction has no on-chain proof: your BTC lands with the solver, who then releases your payout in a single atomic transaction on the destination chain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What protects you here is the signed quote and the refund path, not cryptography. If the payout cannot land at the quoted terms, the solver re-quotes and retries for about an hour, and then the order settles as a refund of BTC to the refund address you supplied when creating the order, as the &lt;a href="https://docs.gobob.xyz/gateway/faq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gateway FAQ&lt;/a&gt; describes (checked 30 October 2026). So in this direction, between your deposit confirming and the payout or refund landing, you are trusting one solver to perform. The exposure is narrow, one order and one counterparty rather than every user's funds at once, but it is real.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  BOB Gateway when you swap into BTC
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The other direction is built differently, and it is where the stronger guarantee lives. Your funds, held as USDT on Ethereum, are escrowed in the GatewayOfframpRegistryV7 contract together with the minimum BTC output you agreed to. If you start from another chain or token, it is first swapped and bridged into that USDT, and a Gateway contract creates the order for you. The solver then pays BTC to your Bitcoin address and must prove it: the payment carries a commitment to your order, and BOB's &lt;a href="https://docs.gobob.xyz/gateway/technical" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LightRelay light client&lt;/a&gt; verifies the transaction and its proof of work on-chain before anything moves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only after that proof checks out does the escrow release your USDT to the solver. If the BTC payout never lands, the full escrow is returned to you once an on-chain refund delay has passed. That delay is configurable between 12 hours and 7 days, and the refund is operated by BOB rather than triggered by you; the &lt;a href="https://docs.gobob.xyz/gateway/faq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FAQ&lt;/a&gt; is plain that neither users nor integrators can trigger a refund themselves. The escrowed funds have exactly two exits: to the solver against a valid proof, or back to you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Can native BTC be moved cross-chain without relying on a centralized bridge custodian?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, and BOB Gateway is only one of several designs that manage it. Each design removes the central custodian and replaces it with a different thing to trust.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Garden Finance settles swaps with hash time-locked contracts on both chains. You lock funds on the source chain and a solver locks funds on the destination chain; revealing a secret redeems both sides, and if either side fails to complete its part, the swap reverts and funds return to their owners. Garden's &lt;a href="https://garden.finance/faq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FAQ&lt;/a&gt; stresses that no third party, including Garden itself, ever takes custody, and that a deposit not confirmed within one hour is cancelled and refunded. Solvers must also stake 210,000 SEED as collateral that can be slashed if they misbehave or go inactive, so a quiet solver costs you time rather than money, and the timelock lets you reclaim your own side without anyone's permission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;THORChain and Chainflip take the opposite approach: shared vaults run by validator sets. On THORChain, your BTC goes into an Asgard vault whose key is split across a shard of node operators, 20 by default, and a supermajority of at least 67 percent must sign before funds move, according to its &lt;a href="https://dev.thorchain.org/bifrost/vault-behaviors.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;vault documentation&lt;/a&gt;. Operators bond RUNE, and a vault that signs an unauthorised outbound transaction is slashed up to 1.5 times the misused amount. On Chainflip, vaults on each chain are controlled by 150 validators using FROST threshold signatures, and 100 of the 150 must cooperate to sign, as Chainflip explains in its &lt;a href="https://chainflip.io/blog/native-bitcoin-swap-no-wrapped-btc-chainflip-architecture" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;architecture walkthrough&lt;/a&gt;. In both cases your BTC joins a pool that also holds everyone else's, which spreads trust across many parties but concentrates the prize if the threshold cryptography or the validator set fails. THORChain's own history shows that risk is not theoretical: in May 2026 a flaw in its threshold signature implementation let an attacker take about $10.7 million from a vault, a figure reported in &lt;a href="https://www.gobob.xyz/en/blog/safest-bitcoin-bridges-for-native-BTC" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;BOB's custody guide&lt;/a&gt; from THORChain's incident report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Wrapped BTC sits at the far end of the spectrum. With a wrapped token, a custodian holds the real BTC in reserve and mints a token against it on another chain, so what you hold is a claim on that custodian staying solvent and operational, a model Chainflip's &lt;a href="https://chainflip.io/blog/native-bitcoin-swap-no-wrapped-btc-chainflip-architecture" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;architecture article&lt;/a&gt; lays out plainly. That is the arrangement the four swap services above are all trying to avoid, each in its own way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Protocol&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Who holds your funds mid-swap&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What you have to trust&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;If the other side goes quiet&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;BOB Gateway, out of BTC&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The one solver filling your order, in its own Bitcoin wallet&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;That solver, plus the signed quote and automatic refund&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Retries for about an hour, then your BTC is refunded&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;BOB Gateway, into BTC&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A contract escrow holding your USDT on Ethereum&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The contract rules and the on-chain Bitcoin light client&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Escrow returns to you in full after the refund delay&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Garden Finance&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hash time-locked contracts on both chains&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The contract code and one solver per order&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The timelock expires and each side reclaims its own funds&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;THORChain&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A shared Asgard vault&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A supermajority of a node shard and their bonded RUNE&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Signing is retried or reassigned to another vault&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Chainflip&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A shared vault on each chain&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100 of 150 validators under FROST threshold signatures&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The validator set keeps custody as a group; no single operator can act or stall alone&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The table shows that removing the central custodian is the easy part; what differs is who or what you trust instead, and how your money finds its way back when something stalls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  So which is it?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BOB Gateway earns the non-custodial label, with the direction caveat stated at the top. Nothing is pooled, no bridge vault holds everyone's BTC, and you rely on one solver per order rather than a custodian or a signer committee. The swap into BTC is its strongest design, because the solver is paid only against an on-chain proof. The swap out of BTC is its softest spot, because your deposit rests in the solver's wallet until the payout or refund lands. Whichever service you use, ask three questions before you send anything: where do my funds sit, who can move them, and what happens if nobody does. All details above were checked against the linked documentation on 30 October 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a one-line change that makes a malicious scikit-learn model invisible to several popular pickle scanners:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;joblib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dump&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;model.joblib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;compress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;bz2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;No change to the payload. The same &lt;code&gt;__reduce__&lt;/code&gt; gadget that a scanner would catch in a raw &lt;code&gt;.pkl&lt;/code&gt; sails straight through once it is compressed — and &lt;code&gt;joblib.load&lt;/code&gt; still runs it. This is not theoretical: public proofs of concept show a joblib-compressed gadget bypassing &lt;strong&gt;picklescan 1.0.4&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;ModelScan&lt;/strong&gt; with zero detections, and it is one of the 22 pickle loading paths catalogued in &lt;em&gt;The Art of Hide and Seek&lt;/em&gt; (USENIX Security '26, &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.19774" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;arXiv:2508.19774&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why it works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A pickle scanner reads opcodes. It expects the file to begin with the pickle &lt;code&gt;PROTO&lt;/code&gt; opcode (&lt;code&gt;80 04&lt;/code&gt;) or a protocol-0 opcode like &lt;code&gt;c&lt;/code&gt; (GLOBAL). But &lt;code&gt;joblib.dump(..., compress=...)&lt;/code&gt; writes the &lt;strong&gt;compressor's&lt;/strong&gt; magic bytes first:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;codec&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;leading bytes&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;zlib&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;78 9c&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;78 da&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;gzip&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;1f 8b&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;bzip2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;42 5a 68&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;BZh&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;xz&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;fd 37 7a 58 5a 00&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;lz4&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;04 22 4d 18&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Point &lt;code&gt;pickletools.genops&lt;/code&gt; at &lt;code&gt;78 9c ...&lt;/code&gt; and it fails at offset 0: that is not a valid opcode, so the scanner reports "no issues found". Meanwhile joblib integrates six decompression backends and peels the wrapper &lt;strong&gt;transparently&lt;/strong&gt; before unpickling. The loader sees a pickle; the scanner saw compressor noise. A tool that routes purely on file extension (&lt;code&gt;.joblib&lt;/code&gt; → pickle scanner) or on pickle magic has a blind spot exactly the width of a &lt;code&gt;zlib.compress&lt;/code&gt; call.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The fix is to decompress like the loader does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The remedy is simple and symmetric: if the loader transparently decompresses before unpickling, the scanner has to as well. I added &lt;code&gt;joblib_pickle_scan.py&lt;/code&gt; to the open-source &lt;a href="https://github.com/munimtechnologies/keras-model-scan" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;keras-model-scan&lt;/a&gt; toolkit. It sniffs the compressor magic, peels up to four stdlib-decompressible layers (zlib/gzip/bz2/xz) &lt;strong&gt;in memory&lt;/strong&gt; — bounded, bomb-guarded — and then disassembles the inner pickle with &lt;code&gt;pickletools&lt;/code&gt;. It never imports joblib, never writes to disk, and never unpickles or executes anything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;JLB001 (high, CWE-502):&lt;/strong&gt; a compression-wrapped stream decompresses to a pickle that imports a code-execution callable. The one opcode-only scanners miss.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;JLB010 (medium):&lt;/strong&gt; a compression-wrapped pickle with a real object graph but no dangerous import — still a pickle a magic/extension router skips.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;JLB012 (medium):&lt;/strong&gt; an lz4-frame container. lz4 is not in the standard library, so the scanner reports it as &lt;em&gt;unscannable&lt;/em&gt; rather than guessing — &lt;code&gt;joblib.load&lt;/code&gt; with the lz4 backend would still read it.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python joblib_pickle_scan.py model.joblib model.pkl.gz
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;One detail worth calling out: I deliberately do &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; match the legacy &lt;code&gt;lzma&lt;/code&gt; "alone" format on its magic byte, because its first byte &lt;code&gt;0x5d&lt;/code&gt; collides with the pickle &lt;code&gt;EMPTY_LIST&lt;/code&gt; opcode (&lt;code&gt;]&lt;/code&gt;). Matching it would turn an ordinary list-first pickle into a false positive. The xz format has an unambiguous six-byte signature, so that is the one worth keying on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Honest limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is static source review, not exploitation. It flags the compression wrapper and the imports inside; it does not prove a given file is exploitable and runs no proof of concept. It peels at most four layers and lz4 frames are reported, not opened. The nuanced top-level-package gadget classification lives in sibling modules in the same repo. The takeaway is small and, I think, underserved: &lt;strong&gt;a compressed pickle is still a pickle, and &lt;code&gt;joblib.load&lt;/code&gt; treats the wrapper as transparent — so your scanner should too.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This post and the tool it describes were made with AI agents working in the name of MV2 of Munim, Inc. The work is open source under Apache-2.0. If you spot a mistake, the repo is the place to raise it.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. BOB Gateway can move native BTC directly to Base: you send bitcoin from your own Bitcoin wallet in one transaction and receive ETH, USDC, oUSDT, cbBTC, EURC or tBTC on Base, with no exchange account and no wrapping step on your side, according to &lt;a href="https://www.gobob.xyz/en/blog/how-to-move-native-btc-across-chains-without-wrapping-or-a-cex" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;BOB's route guide for moving native BTC across chains&lt;/a&gt;. One clarification matters: native BTC itself never leaves the Bitcoin network, so what lands on Base is the asset you chose, not bitcoin in its original form.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What "directly" means here
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Native BTC only exists on Bitcoin. When any service says it moves BTC "to Base", it means you send BTC on Bitcoin and receive a different asset on Base: a swap outcome such as ETH or a stablecoin, or a BTC-backed token such as cbBTC or tBTC, depending on the quote you accept. &lt;a href="https://www.gobob.xyz/en/blog/how-to-move-native-btc-across-chains-without-wrapping-or-a-cex" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;BOB's route guide&lt;/a&gt; makes the same distinction between a different asset out, a BTC-backed token out, and an already wrapped token moved between chains.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two practical points follow. First, you do not need to wrap your BTC yourself or open an exchange account: the exchange route takes four steps across two accounts (deposit, sell, buy, withdraw), while a direct route takes one Bitcoin transaction. Second, Gateway takes destination gas out of the swap quote, so you do not need to hold ETH on Base before you can receive your asset there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How a BOB Gateway swap to Base works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gateway is an intent-based swap protocol, not a lock-and-mint bridge. It does not hold your BTC in a bridge contract and issue a token against it. A BTC swap works in five steps, per &lt;a href="https://www.gobob.xyz/en/blog/best-bitcoin-bridges-for-native-btc-in-2026" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;BOB's guide to the best Bitcoin bridges for native BTC&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You request a quote. Gateway returns a signed quote with the routing, the fees and your guaranteed minimum output.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You create an order and get a unique Bitcoin deposit address for that order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You sign one Bitcoin transaction to that address.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Once the deposit confirms, the solver delivers your output in a single atomic transaction. If it cannot, your BTC is refunded.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The asset arrives in your wallet on Base.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The refund side is spelled out in the &lt;a href="https://docs.gobob.xyz/gateway/faq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gateway FAQ&lt;/a&gt;: if the payout cannot land at the quoted terms, the solver re-quotes and retries with backoff for about an hour, then settles the order as a refund of BTC to the refund address on your order. In the FAQ's words, no user is ever left with funds spent and nothing delivered. Total time depends mostly on the Bitcoin confirmation, which the FAQ puts at typically 10 to 60 minutes, plus delivery on the destination chain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Which Bitcoin bridge supports moving native BTC directly to Base?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BOB Gateway is not the only route. I checked the main alternatives against their own documentation on 30 October 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Garden Finance&lt;/strong&gt; also takes native BTC to Base. Garden lists Base among its supported chains and cbBTC among the assets it swaps, and its swaps settle through hash time-locked contracts (HTLCs) on both chains, according to &lt;a href="https://garden.finance/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Garden's site and FAQ&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;a href="https://www.gobob.xyz/en/blog/how-to-move-native-btc-across-chains-without-wrapping-or-a-cex" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;BOB's route guide&lt;/a&gt; notes that Garden delivering cbBTC on Base is the route that Base's own documentation lists for getting BTC onto Base.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;THORChain&lt;/strong&gt; reaches Base as well. Its &lt;a href="https://docs.thorchain.org/what-is-thorchain-and-rune.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;official documentation&lt;/a&gt; lists Base among its supported blockchains and USDC among the stablecoin pools on Base. The design is different: you swap through liquidity pools held in vaults that node operators control with threshold signatures, rather than accepting a solver's signed quote. Its docs also warn that individual chains are sometimes paused, so check the live status first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NEAR Intents&lt;/strong&gt; can route BTC to Base, but with an extra step in the middle. Your BTC is credited on NEAR through its Omni Bridge or PoA Bridge, solvers settle the swap on NEAR, and the output is then withdrawn to the destination chain, as described in &lt;a href="https://www.gobob.xyz/en/blog/best-bitcoin-bridges-for-native-btc-in-2026" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;BOB's bridge comparison&lt;/a&gt;. NEAR's own documentation lists both Bitcoin and Base (chain ID 8453) as supported networks for Intents, per the &lt;a href="https://docs.neartreasury.com/payments/intents" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NEAR Treasury docs on NEAR Intents&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Chainflip&lt;/strong&gt; cannot do this route at all. Its &lt;a href="https://docs.chainflip.io/protocol/supported-chains-assets/chains-assets" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;supported chains and assets documentation&lt;/a&gt;, last updated 21 September 2026, lists Ethereum, Bitcoin, Arbitrum, Polkadot, Solana, Assethub, Tron and BSC. Base is not on the list. Chainflip remains a reasonable option for native swaps into its own chains, but if your destination is Base, it is out.&lt;/p&gt;

