&lt;h2&gt;


  What you end up holding matters more than the route
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The six Gateway outputs on Base fall into two groups, and the difference matters after the swap is over.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ETH, USDC, oUSDT and EURC are swap outcomes. You sold your BTC, nobody is holding bitcoin on your behalf, and you no longer have BTC price exposure. That is the right result if your goal is to spend, trade or earn on Base.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;cbBTC and tBTC are BTC-backed tokens. You keep BTC price exposure, but your claim on the underlying bitcoin now runs through an issuer or a signer set. cbBTC is Coinbase's wrapped bitcoin token, built for Base. tBTC is a 1:1 BTC-backed token secured by Threshold's 51-of-100 threshold signer model, which you can redeem for BTC later, as set out in &lt;a href="https://www.gobob.xyz/en/blog/best-bitcoin-bridges-for-native-btc-in-2026" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;BOB's bridge comparison&lt;/a&gt;. Picking one of these is a custody decision, not just a route decision, so make it deliberately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Which route should you pick?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the widest choice of assets on Base and a guaranteed minimum output shown before you send anything, BOB Gateway is the most complete option right now. Routes change, so confirm your exact asset in the &lt;a href="https://docs.gobob.xyz/gateway/supported-routes" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gateway supported routes documentation&lt;/a&gt; on the day you swap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Garden Finance is the better fit if you specifically want cbBTC on Base and you value a refund path you claim yourself after the timelock, instead of relying on the protocol to refund you. THORChain fits better if you prefer pool-based native swaps secured by a validator set over a solver quote model, and you are comfortable checking that the Base chain is not paused. NEAR Intents makes sense if you already work inside its ecosystem and want its wider chain reach, and you accept the extra bridge step through NEAR.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One last caution on price. BOB's published quote tests from 14 September 2026 compared these protocols on Ethereum routes, not on Base, so do not assume the same ranking holds for BTC to Base. Quotes move with the market, so compare a live quote for your exact amount on at least two services before you send BTC.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;IntersectionObserver is great for lazy-loading, but the tricky part is deciding when to start loading. If you wait until the element is visible, users see pop-in. If you start too early, you waste bandwidth. This adaptation explains a small, testable pattern that separates three concerns: when to notice, which visibility milestone counts, and how to make the decision stable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Implementation summary (the server will insert the verified code example): the preload wrapper forwards root and rootMargin into the IntersectionObserver constructor, passes a threshold array as the 'threshold' option, and stores per-element state in a WeakMap. When the observer reports an intersection, the wrapper schedules a microtask or short timer and then rechecks the element's bounding box and a shouldLoad predicate; if the element still has non-zero area and the predicate allows it, loadFn is called exactly once for that element.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Expected behavior&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Early-but-not-too-early: a positive bottom rootMargin (px or %) starts prefetch while the element is still approaching the viewport, giving the asset time to be ready when the user reaches it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Single-firing: thresholds plus a WeakMap 'loaded' flag ensure loadFn runs just once per target even if the observer callbacks oscillate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resistance to layout jitter: a microtask/timer debounce and a width/height &amp;gt; 0 check avoid starting loads for elements that briefly appear due to reflow, device pixel rounding, or resize.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choosing units and thresholds&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pixel rootMargin: use when you need a fixed lead (predictable on a known layout).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Percentage rootMargin: use when the lead should scale with the root/container height (responsive layouts).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Threshold array: pick a single threshold (e.g., 0.25) for a one-shot trigger or a small array when you need more diagnostic crossings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Limitations&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The wrapper and tests prove the decision logic (option forwarding, single-fire behavior, jitter reduction) but do not simulate browser intersection geometry, network timing, or device-specific scrolling feel. You must tune rootMargin and thresholds in the browsers/devices you support.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The wrapper does not handle caching, connection prioritization, or post-fetch behavior — those are separate decisions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why use this pattern&lt;br&gt;
Separating rootMargin (lead), thresholds (milestone), and a stability debounce (correctness guard) yields a predictable preload trigger that reduces pop-in and avoids duplicate loads. See the server-inserted example and the article for tests that demonstrate the contract and how to adapt it to your components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Working example
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createPreloadObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rootMargin&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;0px 0px 0px 0px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;thresholds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;debounceMs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;shouldLoad&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;loadFn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;IntersectionObserverClass&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;globalThis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;IntersectionObserverClass&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;TypeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;IntersectionObserverClass must be provided&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Map from element -&amp;gt; state&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;elementState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;WeakMap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Microtask / short timer debounce helper: returns a canceller&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;scheduleCheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;elementState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// already scheduled&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Use microtask debounce if debounceMs === 0, otherwise setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;debounceMs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;performStableCheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;performStableCheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;debounceMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;elementState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A layout-stable check: confirm element still has a non-zero area and&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// that shouldLoad still returns true. Consumers can provide an element&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// with a getBoundingClientRect method (like DOM elements) or a&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// plain object that implements that method in tests.&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;performStableCheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;getBoundingClientRect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getBoundingClientRect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// If rect exists but area is zero, consider it not stable / not visible.&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

      &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;shouldLoad&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ensure we only call load once per element&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;elementState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{};&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;loaded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;loaded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;elementState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

      &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadFn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Swallow to avoid unhandled promise rejections during microtask&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// but rethrow synchronously so tests can see mistakes when desired.&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserverClass&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Only schedule a preload when IntersectionObserver reports&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// that the element is intersecting (isIntersecting true) OR&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// when a threshold crossing happened that implies we passed&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// a numeric threshold. The IO API guarantees callbacks only&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// happen when thresholds are crossed or intersection state&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// flips, so checking isIntersecting is usually enough for&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// a preload-before-visible strategy.&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nf"&gt;scheduleCheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rootMargin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;threshold&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;thresholds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;observe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;elementState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;loaded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;observe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;unobserve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;unobserve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;elementState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;delete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;disconnect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;disconnect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// expose internals for test inspection only (not for production use)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;_observerInstance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Read the full article: &lt;a href="https://chriseugenerodriguez.com/blog/intersectionobserver-preload-without-jitter" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://chriseugenerodriguez.com/blog/intersectionobserver-preload-without-jitter&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every project I have worked on that used a Redis-style store ended up with a folder of tiny Lua scripts. They were never complicated. Read a number, compare it with something, write one or two keys, return. But each of them had to be loaded, called by its SHA, kept in step with the application code, and explained to the next person who opened the folder.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I started Ruvio I went through that folder and asked a plain question for each script: &lt;em&gt;is this a command that nobody has written yet?&lt;/em&gt; Surprisingly often the answer was yes. This post is the list of the ones that made it into the server, with the exact replies you get back. Everything below was run against the 0.4.0 release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Move money only if there is enough
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The classic one. Take 30 credits from a wallet and put them in escrow, but only if the wallet holds at least 30. Nobody should ever see the money in both places, or in neither.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;SET {user:42}:wallet 50
TRANSFER {user:42}:wallet {user:42}:escrow 30
# 1) 20  -&amp;gt; wallet after
# 2) 30  -&amp;gt; escrow after
TRANSFER {user:42}:wallet {user:42}:escrow 30
# (nil)  -&amp;gt; only 20 left, nothing changed
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Both balances change in one WAL record, so a crash cannot leave you with only half of the move on disk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keep both keys in one slot&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;TRANSFER&lt;/code&gt; works on two keys of the same slot. The &lt;code&gt;{user:42}&lt;/code&gt; hash tag in the key names is what puts them there, in a single server as well as in a cluster.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the balances are fields of one hash, &lt;code&gt;HTRANSFER account:7 available held 30 MAX 1000&lt;/code&gt; does the same between two fields and also refuses if the destination would pass the cap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Sell the last ticket once
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;DECRBY&lt;/code&gt; happily goes below zero, so the usual fix is a script that reads the stock first. &lt;code&gt;TAKE&lt;/code&gt; subtracts only when the key holds enough:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;SET stock:ticket-42 500
TAKE stock:ticket-42 2
# 498   -&amp;gt; took 2, 498 left
TAKE stock:ticket-42 1000
# (nil) -&amp;gt; not enough, the key is unchanged
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The mirror image is a counter with a ceiling, for seats in a room or members in a team:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;INCRBY seats:room-9 1 MAX 120
# 57    -&amp;gt; joined
# (nil) -&amp;gt; room is full, nothing was written
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  3. A daily counter that cleans up after itself
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This one is so small it hurts. You want a per-day counter that disappears tomorrow. With plain &lt;code&gt;INCR&lt;/code&gt; you need a second &lt;code&gt;EXPIRE&lt;/code&gt; call, and if you only want to set the deadline when the key is created, you are back to a script or a &lt;code&gt;MULTI&lt;/code&gt; block.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;INCR hits:user-7:2026-10-29 EX 86400
# 1     -&amp;gt; first hit, the day's deadline is set
# 2, 3… -&amp;gt; later hits leave the deadline alone
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;EX&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;PX&lt;/code&gt; on &lt;code&gt;INCR&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;INCRBY&lt;/code&gt; only apply when the call creates the counter. Later calls do not push the deadline forward.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Keep the newest value, whatever order writers arrive in
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Event consumers that run in parallel love to write an older watermark after a newer one. &lt;code&gt;IFGT&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;IFLT&lt;/code&gt; on &lt;code&gt;SET&lt;/code&gt; compare against the stored integer and only write when the new number wins:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;SET watermark:orders 1700000200 IFGT
# OK    -&amp;gt; written
SET watermark:orders 1700000100 IFGT
# (nil) -&amp;gt; stored number is greater, nothing written
SET price:best 499 IFLT PX 3600000
# OK    -&amp;gt; new lowest price, expires in an hour
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Integers only&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
A stored value that is not a number is an error and stays as it was. I prefer that to a silent string comparison.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. A rate limit in one round trip
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fixed window, sliding window and token bucket are all &lt;code&gt;LIMIT&lt;/code&gt;. The reply is the allowance that is left, and a refused call is not counted:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;LIMIT api:key-7 3 60000
# 2
# 1
# 0
# (nil) -&amp;gt; window is full, this call was not counted
LIMIT api:key-7:burst 10 1000 TOKENS COST 2 WITHRETRY
# 1) 8  -&amp;gt; 8 tokens left
# 2) 0  -&amp;gt; no need to wait
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;WITHRETRY&lt;/code&gt; puts the wait in the reply, so the number for a &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt; header comes from the server rather than from a guess in the client.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  6. Pop the jobs that are due
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sorted set with the run time as the score is the simplest scheduler there is. The awkward part was always "give me what is due &lt;em&gt;and remove it&lt;/em&gt;" without two workers picking the same job.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ZADD {jobs}:due 100 job:9 200 job:10 300 job:11
ZPOPBYSCORE {jobs}:due 150 COUNT 10
# job:9 -&amp;gt; due and removed in the same call
ZMOVEDUE {jobs}:due {jobs}:ready 250 COUNT 100
# 1     -&amp;gt; job:10 moved to the tail of the list {jobs}:ready
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;ZMOVEDUE&lt;/code&gt; hands due members to a list queue in one recorded step, up to 10,000 per call. Both keys must share a slot; without the &lt;code&gt;{jobs}&lt;/code&gt; tag the server answers &lt;code&gt;CROSSSLOT&lt;/code&gt;, as it should. There is a whole post on &lt;a href="https://ruviodb.com/blog/delayed-jobs-without-a-scheduler/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;delayed jobs&lt;/a&gt; if you want the stream version too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  7. Save only if nobody else saved first
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two people open the same profile, both press save. With &lt;code&gt;SETV&lt;/code&gt; every write returns a version and a write that names an old version is refused:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;SETV profile:ada "v1"
# 1
SETV profile:ada "v2" 1
# 2
SETV profile:ada "v3" 1
# ERR version mismatch
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;No &lt;code&gt;WATCH&lt;/code&gt;, no retry loop around &lt;code&gt;MULTI&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I did not turn into commands
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not every script deserves to become a command. Anything with real business rules, several branches and a dozen keys is still better as code in your application, or as a script if you really need it next to the data. Ruvio runs Lua for exactly that. The commands above made the cut because they are small, they come up in almost every codebase, and they share three properties:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;All or nothing.&lt;/strong&gt; Either the whole thing happens or nothing is written, not to the key and not to the log.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One record.&lt;/strong&gt; A refused call leaves no trace, and an accepted one is a single WAL record that replicas apply the same way.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A typed method.&lt;/strong&gt; Each one is a method in all six clients, so there is no script text to send and no reply to parse by hand.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is also a speed side. Every one of these is measured against the script it replaces on the &lt;a href="https://ruviodb.com/benchmarks/#scripted" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;benchmarks page&lt;/a&gt;, together with the memory each key takes. I will let the table speak there rather than paste numbers here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All six first-party clients have a typed method for each command, so in C# the first example is &lt;code&gt;await db.TransferAsync(...)&lt;/code&gt; and not a string you send and parse. The &lt;a href="https://ruviodb.com/blog/one-command-instead-of-a-script/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;original post&lt;/a&gt; has tabs under each example with the same steps in all six languages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I used Claude to help edit this post; the commands and outputs were run by me against the 0.4.0 release.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://ruviodb.com/blog/one-command-instead-of-a-script/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ruviodb.com&lt;/a&gt;. Ruvio is open source: &lt;a href="https://github.com/salihcantekin/ruvio" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/salihcantekin/ruvio&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Rotary Position Embeddings: How Rotating Queries and Keys Encodes Relative Position
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Transformers have no recurrence and no convolution, so on their own they cannot tell whether a token came first, fifth, or five-hundredth. Early transformers solved this by adding a position vector to each token embedding. That works, but it mixes "what the token means" with "where the token sits" in a single sum, and it forces the model to learn how position should interact with content from data alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rotary Position Embeddings, introduced as RoFormer in 2021, take a different route. Instead of adding position, RoPE rotates the query and key vectors by an angle that depends on their absolute position. The beautiful consequence is that the attention score between two tokens then depends only on their relative distance. Absolute position goes in, relative position comes out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The core idea in one picture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine each query and key as a vector in a plane. Before attention is computed, rotate the query for position m by angle m times theta, and rotate the key for position n by angle n times theta. When you take their dot product, the two rotations partly cancel, and what survives depends only on the difference m minus n.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the whole trick. Nothing is added to the token embedding itself. The value vectors are left untouched, so position changes how tokens attend to each other, not what information they carry once attended to.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The maths: rotation matrices and relative attention
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the standard attention score between a query at position m and a key at position n, before any position encoding:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;

&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="katex-element"&gt;
  &lt;span class="katex-display"&gt;&lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord text"&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mpunct"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mrel"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;RoPE replaces the raw query and key with rotated versions. Let R(m) be a rotation by angle m times theta. Then:&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="katex-element"&gt;
  &lt;span class="katex-display"&gt;&lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;′&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mrel"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mpunct"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;′&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mrel"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;The new attention score is:&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="katex-element"&gt;
  &lt;span class="katex-display"&gt;&lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;′&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;′&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mrel"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mrel"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mbin"&gt;−&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;The last step uses the defining property of rotations: rotating by n and then un-rotating by m is the same as rotating by n minus m. The score is therefore a function of the query content, the key content, and the relative offset n minus m, but not of m or n separately. This is exactly the property a relative position encoding should have, and RoPE gets it by construction rather than by learning it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In two dimensions, the rotation matrix is the familiar one:&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="katex-element"&gt;
  &lt;span class="katex-display"&gt;&lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mrel"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="minner"&gt;&lt;span class="mopen delimcenter"&gt;&lt;span class="delimsizing size1"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mtable"&gt;&lt;span class="col-align-c"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mop"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;θ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="arraycolsep"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="arraycolsep"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="col-align-c"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;−&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mop"&gt;sin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;θ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mop"&gt;sin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;θ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="arraycolsep"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="arraycolsep"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="col-align-c"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mop"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;θ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose delimcenter"&gt;&lt;span class="delimsizing size1"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;


  From two dimensions to the full model
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Real query and key vectors have hundreds of dimensions, not two. RoPE handles this by splitting the vector into pairs of coordinates and rotating each pair by a different frequency. For a vector of dimension d, pair i (counting from 0) is rotated by angle m times theta_i, where:&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="katex-element"&gt;
  &lt;span class="katex-display"&gt;&lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;θ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mrel"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;−&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;The lowest pairs rotate fast with position, so they are sensitive to small changes in distance. The highest pairs rotate slowly, so they change gradually over long distances. Together, the pairs give the model a multi-scale sense of distance, much like the sine and cosine waves of the original absolute position encoding, but applied multiplicatively through rotation instead of additively.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is an equivalent complex-number view that makes implementation cleaner. Treat each pair (x_0, x_1) as a complex number x_0 plus i times x_1. Rotating by m times theta_i is then just multiplication by the complex exponential:&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="katex-element"&gt;
  &lt;span class="katex-display"&gt;&lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mpunct"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mrel"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mbin"&gt;⋅&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;im&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;θ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal mtight"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;This is why RoPE implementations in libraries often work with complex tensors or with interleaved sine and cosine multiplications. The two views are the same operation written in different notation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why rotation beats addition
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Absolute sinusoidal encodings add a position vector p_m to the token embedding x_m. Expand the attention score and you get four terms: content-content, content-position, position-content, and position-position. The model must learn to disentangle them, and the interaction between content and position is unconstrained.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;RoPE keeps content and position separate by design. Position acts only through the rotation applied to queries and keys, and the resulting score is provably a function of relative distance. Three practical benefits follow:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No position vector to learn or to extrapolate badly.&lt;/strong&gt; The rotation formula is defined for any integer position, so in principle there is no hard maximum length baked into a lookup table.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Attention decay with distance emerges naturally.&lt;/strong&gt; As the relative distance grows, the rotations for different frequency pairs drift out of phase, and the expected attention score between unrelated tokens tends to shrink. This gives the model a soft bias toward nearby tokens without a hard attention window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Values stay position-free.&lt;/strong&gt; Because values are not rotated, a token contributes the same content wherever it sits. Position governs routing (who attends to whom), not payload (what is passed along).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A minimal implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The code below shows the idea for one pair of coordinates, using the complex-number view. Real implementations vectorise this across all pairs and all heads, but the logic is identical.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;apply_rope_pair&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x_pair&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;positions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;theta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# x_pair: (..., 2) real tensor holding (x0, x1) for one pair
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# positions: (...) integer positions
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# theta: scalar frequency for this pair
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;angles&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;positions&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;theta&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# shape (...,)
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;angles&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;unsqueeze&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;sin&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;angles&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;unsqueeze&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;x0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x_pair&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[...,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x_pair&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[...,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;rotated_0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sin&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;rotated_1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sin&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rotated_0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rotated_1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Example: rotate a query at position 5 and a key at position 8
# by the same frequency, then check that only the gap (3) matters.
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tensor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;1.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tensor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;1.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;theta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.5&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;q_rot&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;apply_rope_pair&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tensor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;theta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;k_rot&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;apply_rope_pair&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tensor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;theta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;score&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;q_rot&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;k_rot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# score equals cos((8 - 5) * theta) = cos(1.5), as the theory predicts
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tensor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;1.5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two implementation details matter in practice. First, RoPE is applied to queries and keys only, after their linear projections and before the attention dot product. Applying it to values is a common bug that changes what tokens transmit rather than how they are routed. Second, the pairing convention matters: some implementations pair adjacent coordinates (0 with 1, 2 with 3), while others pair coordinates half a dimension apart (the "GPT-NeoX" style). Both are valid rotations, but mixing them up between training and inference silently changes the encoding, so the convention must match the pretrained weights.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Length extrapolation: where the theory meets reality
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;RoPE is often described as giving free length extrapolation, because the rotation formula works for any position. In practice, extrapolation is much messier. A model trained on sequences of length 2,048 has only ever seen relative distances up to 2,048, and the slow-rotating pairs have barely completed a fraction of a turn within that range. Feed it a sequence of length 32,768 and those pairs enter angle ranges never seen in training, while the attention softmax faces many more competing tokens than it was calibrated for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is why a family of RoPE scaling methods exists. They all adjust the frequencies theta_i, or equivalently the base 10000, to stretch the rotation schedule over longer contexts:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Linear position interpolation&lt;/strong&gt; rescales positions by the ratio of new length to old length, so a 32,768-token context is squeezed back into the familiar 2,048 range. It works surprisingly well with a little fine-tuning but blurs fine-grained distance distinctions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;NTK-aware scaling&lt;/strong&gt; changes the base instead of the positions, stretching low frequencies more than high ones, on the reasoning that high-frequency pairs already complete many turns within training length and need less adjustment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;YaRN&lt;/strong&gt; combines frequency rescaling with a temperature adjustment to the attention softmax, compensating for the entropy growth that comes from attending over many more tokens.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lesson is not that RoPE fails at long context, but that extrapolation is a property of the whole trained system - frequencies, attention calibration, and training distribution - not of the rotation formula alone. If you extend context, expect to validate on genuinely long inputs, not just to trust the formula.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Limitations and common pitfalls
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;RoPE is elegant, but it is not a complete theory of position. Keep these limits in mind:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Relative is not always enough.&lt;/strong&gt; Tasks that need absolute position, such as counting from the start of a sequence or locating the tenth item, get no direct absolute signal from RoPE alone. Models recover some of it indirectly, but it is not guaranteed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The decay is a tendency, not a guarantee.&lt;/strong&gt; The argument that attention decays with distance holds in expectation over random content vectors. For specific learned queries and keys, long-distance attention can still be strong, which is sometimes exactly what you want.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Head dimension must be even.&lt;/strong&gt; Pairs need two coordinates each. An odd head dimension forces padding or a different scheme, and silently dropping a coordinate changes the model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;RoPE interacts with attention variants.&lt;/strong&gt; With grouped-query attention or multi-query attention, the same rotation schedule applies to shared keys, which is fine, but the reduced number of key heads changes how much positional diversity the model can express.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Do not confuse RoPE with ALiBi.&lt;/strong&gt; ALiBi adds a fixed linear penalty proportional to distance directly to the attention score. RoPE rotates vectors so distance emerges from geometry. Both encode relative position, but they are different mechanisms with different extrapolation behaviour, and they are sometimes combined rather than substituted.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When to reach for RoPE
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;RoPE is now the default position encoding in most large language models for good reason: it is simple, parameter-free, provably relative, and cheap to compute. Reach for it when you want position to shape attention routing without polluting token content, and when you need a principled starting point for long-context scaling. Reach for something else, or an addition to RoPE, when your task genuinely needs absolute position, hard distance cutoffs, or a position signal that survives into the values themselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deeper lesson of RoPE is a design principle worth stealing: instead of asking a model to learn a structural property from data, build that property into the operation itself. Additive position encodings ask the model to discover that attention should depend on relative distance. RoPE guarantees it, and leaves the model's capacity for everything else.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Connect with me on LinkedIn:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/umeshtharukaofficial/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;linkedin.com/in/umeshtharukaofficial&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your onchain executor can pass its unit tests, produce valid calldata, and still send funds through an unintended path. The difficult part is not writing more tests. It is knowing what each test environment proves and where its evidence stops.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful testing ladder has distinct stages: pure simulation, forked-chain execution, a production canary using a trivial amount, and staged production use. None is a substitute for the others.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most dangerous design is often a &lt;code&gt;dryRun&lt;/code&gt; boolean scattered across the codebase. If some transaction paths respect it while another path broadcasts, the dry run creates false confidence. A system with no dry-run mode is visibly dangerous. A partially enforced one looks safe until the missed branch moves funds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Start with a deterministic execution plan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Separate deciding what to do from doing it. The planner should consume explicit inputs and produce an execution plan without holding signing or broadcast capabilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A plan might contain:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;chain identifier&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;contract and token addresses&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;calldata&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;value to send&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;approval requirements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;slippage bounds&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;deadline&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;expected state transitions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;reasons the action should be rejected&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same planner should run in simulation, fork tests, canaries, and production. Environment-specific code should sit behind the transport boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Conceptually:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;market snapshot + strategy state
              |
              v
       deterministic planner
              |
              v
       validated transaction plan
              |
      +-------+--------+
      |                |
 simulation transport  live transport
 no signing material   signing + broadcast
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is stronger than checking a flag immediately before an RPC call. In simulation mode, the process should not load a signer capable of broadcasting. The restriction is structural rather than conventional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pure planning tests are good at checking arithmetic, rounding, token decimals, state transitions, allocation rules, and invariants such as “a rejected plan cannot contain an executable transaction.” Property-based tests are useful here because many failures live at boundaries: zero balances, minimum trade sizes, stale timestamps, or values just inside a slippage limit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What this layer does badly is represent external state. A mock contract behaves as its author expects, not necessarily as the deployed contract behaves. A simulated quote does not expose an RPC outage, a changed pool state, or a transaction competing in the public mempool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Use a fork to test contract interactions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A forked chain lets the executor interact with deployed bytecode and a captured state snapshot. This catches errors that pure simulation tends to miss:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;incorrect ABI encoding&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;approval and allowance assumptions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;token decimal mistakes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;revert conditions in deployed contracts&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;multi-call ordering problems&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;unexpected behavior from non-standard tokens&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fork tests should execute the complete sequence, not isolated calls. If production performs an approval followed by a swap, the test should run that sequence through the same transaction-building code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pin the block used by the test. Without a pinned state, a passing test can become a failing test because liquidity, balances, or contract state changed rather than because the code changed. It is also useful to maintain fixtures for awkward historical states instead of testing only a convenient recent snapshot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A fork still does not reproduce production. It usually omits real RPC latency, provider rate limits, signer failures, relayer behavior, gas sponsorship, mempool ordering, and reorgs. Local mining also tends to be more predictable than public block production. A fork proves that a transaction can execute under the captured state. It does not prove that the transaction will execute under the state present when production broadcasts it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make dry run a capability boundary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A safe dry run should be incapable of broadcasting, not merely instructed not to.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the live signer and broadcast client out of the dry-run process. Give that process a simulation transport that supports calls and gas estimation but has no method or credentials for sending transactions. If a code path unexpectedly requests a broadcast, it should fail closed and emit an observable error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then test the boundary itself. Exercise every action type through dry-run mode and assert that no live transport was constructed. Include cancellation, retries, approval refreshes, cleanup jobs, and administrative paths. Background workers are easy to overlook because the visible strategy path may respect dry run while a retry queue still holds an executable payload.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Audit logs should record the plan, validation decisions, simulation result, selected transport, and whether signing material was loaded. Logs are not an enforcement mechanism, but they make boundary violations easier to diagnose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is duplication pressure. Simulation and live transports can drift. Reduce that risk by sharing the planner, serializer, and validators while keeping the final send capability separate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Use a canary to test the production path
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A canary sends a real transaction using the production RPC, signer, policy checks, monitoring, and confirmation logic, but with the smallest practical exposure permitted by the venue and token precision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This stage can reveal configuration errors that earlier layers cannot: wrong chain selection, unavailable signing credentials, broken gas handling, provider restrictions, stale configuration, and monitoring that watches the wrong address.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A canary is still weak evidence. It covers a particular route under a particular market state. It may not exercise retries, cancellation, partial completion, or adverse ordering. Success should not cause the system to bypass limits or broaden access automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Canaries also need containment. Use a dedicated wallet, strict token permissions, bounded transaction value, and alerts tied to expected transaction hashes and state changes. Stop after unexpected behavior rather than repeatedly retrying an uncertain action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Production needs continuing controls
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a canary, staged activation is safer than treating deployment as the end of testing. Keep explicit limits on value, action frequency, allowed contracts, tokens, and slippage. Monitor both submitted transactions and intended plans so that “nothing happened” is detectable alongside incorrect execution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plan for ambiguous outcomes. An RPC timeout does not establish that a transaction was rejected. Before retrying, inspect the transaction hash, sender nonce, and chain state. Blind retries can duplicate an action when the initial request reached the network but its response did not reach the executor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pausing should block new plans at the scheduler and block broadcasts at the transport boundary. Relying on one check leaves another path available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: I build &lt;a href="https://app.tradevo.co/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=syndicate&amp;amp;utm_content=testing-code-that-spends-real-money" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tradevo&lt;/a&gt;, software where creators describe crypto strategies in plain English, historically test and paper-trade the compiled deterministic rules, submit them for human review, set a free or monthly price, and publish approved strategies, while other users can discover, subscribe to, allocate USDC to, and automatically run those rules from their own wallets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The testing ladder works because each stage answers a narrower question. Simulation checks planning logic. Forks check interaction with captured contract state. Canaries check the real operational path with contained exposure. Production controls limit what happens when assumptions fail. Treating any stage as proof of the next is where expensive mistakes begin.&lt;/p&gt;
